WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.540
Checkpoints can appear in different formats depending on the framework, conversion process, fine-tuning toolchain, quantization workflow, and deployment target.

2
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:17.320
A model package may also include tokenizer files, configuration files, adapter weights, metadata, scripts, or runtime dependencies.

3
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.780
Format decisions therefore belong in the trust discussion.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.420
At a defensive level, the important point is that not every format has the same loading behavior.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:33.320
Some serialization formats can represent more than simple tensor data, and that complexity requires careful handling.

6
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:41.580
Teams should avoid casually loading unfamiliar checkpoint files on sensitive machines, shared infrastructure, or production hosts.

7
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:50.660
Safer tensor-focused formats are preferred when they fit the workflow because they reduce unnecessary loading risk and make artifact contents easier to reason about.

8
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:58.680
This does not remove the need for provenance, integrity checks, evaluation, or access control, but it narrows one part of the intake risk.

9
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.920
Conversion should be treated as a controlled transformation, not a casual cleanup step.

10
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:15.220
The record should include inputs, outputs, tool names, tool versions, settings, environment, person or job responsible, and verification steps.

11
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.000
A converted artifact should not lose the lineage of the source checkpoint.

12
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.200
The safe habit is to know what kind of artifact is being loaded before allowing it into an operational environment.

13
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:36.260
If the team cannot explain the format, source, companion files, conversion path, and intended loader, the checkpoint should remain in a controlled intake path until the evidence is complete enough.
